\title[Arithmetic tameness of the Mukai groups on the superspecial K3]
{Arithmetic tameness of the Mukai maximal symplectic groups and global tameness
of $\Aut(X)$ on the superspecial K3 surface in characteristic $p>11$}

\author{Aristotle}

\date{2026-06-26}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Mukai's theorem classifies the finite groups arising as maximal groups of symplectic
automorphisms of a complex K3 surface: there are exactly eleven. Ohashi--Sch\"utt-type
results show the same eleven groups govern the \emph{superspecial} K3 surface in
characteristic $p$ provided $p$ is large, the standing threshold being $p>11$. The
classification rests on a \emph{tameness} hypothesis: the relevant automorphism order
is prime to the characteristic. We isolate and prove the arithmetic content of this
hypothesis. The eleven Mukai orders are $7$-smooth: their least common multiple is the
single integer $40320=2^{7}\cdot 3^{2}\cdot 5\cdot 7$, every Mukai order divides
$40320$, and hence every prime factor of every Mukai order is at most $7$. Therefore
every prime $p>11$ (indeed $p>7$) is coprime to every Mukai order. Synthesizing with
the characteristic-$p$ tameness of the non-symplectic index $[G:G_s]$ yields
\emph{global tameness}: for the superspecial K3 surface in characteristic $p>11$ with
symplectic subgroup a maximal Mukai group, the order of the entire finite automorphism
group is prime to $p$. The arithmetic is exhausted at $p>7$, which localizes the
residual, still-conjectural rigidity ($[G:G_s]=1$) to the geometric gap between $7$ and
$11$, quantified by the explicit integer $40320$.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Background}
A \emph{K3 surface} $X$ over an algebraically closed field $k$ is a smooth projective
surface with trivial canonical bundle $\omega_X\cong\mathcal{O}_X$ and
$h^1(X,\mathcal{O}_X)=0$. K3 surfaces carry a one-dimensional space of regular
$2$-forms; a generator $\omega_X$ acts as a symplectic form, and the action of an
automorphism on it splits symmetries into two kinds.

Let $G\le\Aut(X)$ be finite. Each $g\in G$ acts on $H^0(X,\omega_X)\cong k$ by a
scalar, defining the \emph{period character}
\[
\chi:G\longrightarrow k^{\times},\qquad g^{*}\omega_X=\chi(g)\,\omega_X .
\]
Its kernel $G_s:=\ker\chi$ is the group of \emph{symplectic} automorphisms; the
quotient $G/G_s\hookrightarrow k^{\times}$ is cyclic, and $n:=[G:G_s]$ is the
\emph{non-symplectic index}. By construction
\begin{equation}\label{eq:factor}
\#G=\#G_s\cdot[G:G_s].
\end{equation}

Mukai's theorem (over $\mathbb{C}$) states that the maximal finite groups of
symplectic automorphisms of a K3 surface are exactly the eleven \emph{Mukai groups} of
Table~\ref{tab:mukai}.

